WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.989
Back to the theatre.

2
00:00:03.989 --> 00:00:07.790
Last time, the lead actor had a stand-in at the stage door.

3
00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:11.979
It worked because the role had a job card: in code, an interface.

4
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.867
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

5
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:18.254
Most of my classes have no interface.

6
00:00:18.254 --> 00:00:20.899
So can anything stand in for them at all?

7
00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:24.889
Take the person who runs the lighting cues.

8
00:00:24.889 --> 00:00:27.674
In code, that's a class called CueRunner.

9
00:00:27.674 --> 00:00:34.469
Its method run runs one cue, such as LX-14, and first calls this.check, to make sure the cue exists.

10
00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:38.071
A third method, stage, is final, and names the stage.

11
00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:41.536
There's no interface anywhere, so Proxy can't help.

12
00:00:41.536 --> 00:00:43.575
It only implements interfaces.

13
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.970
The costume department has an answer.

14
00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:51.041
For a role with no card, they sew a full copy of the costume.

15
00:00:51.041 --> 00:00:58.451
In Java, that costume is a subclass: a class that extends yours and may override, that is replace, its methods.

16
00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:02.254
Here's a timing costume, written by hand.

17
00:01:02.254 --> 00:01:06.408
Its run starts a clock, calls super.run, and logs the time.

18
00:01:06.408 --> 00:01:10.070
super.run is the original body, on this same object.

19
00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.239
check works the same way, and stage is final.

20
00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:15.042
Let's map it.

21
00:01:15.042 --> 00:01:16.928
The costume is the subclass.

22
00:01:16.928 --> 00:01:20.766
Each override is a place to add a step, before and after.

23
00:01:20.766 --> 00:01:23.527
super.run is the role, played as written.

24
00:01:23.527 --> 00:01:28.173
And every caller still sees a CueRunner, because the subclass is one.

25
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.249
Two methods, fine.

26
00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:32.802
Now say forty classes, ten methods each.

27
00:01:32.802 --> 00:01:39.823
That's four hundred overrides, sewn by hand the night before opening, each one a chance to forget the finally.

28
00:01:39.823 --> 00:01:42.951
Wouldn't it be nice if someone sewed them for us?

29
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:48.649
That's Byte Buddy, a library that writes classes while your program runs.

30
00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:50.221
You tell it four things.

31
00:01:50.221 --> 00:01:51.531
The class to extend.

32
00:01:51.531 --> 00:01:53.235
Which methods to override.

33
00:01:53.235 --> 00:01:54.676
Who handles each call.

34
00:01:54.676 --> 00:01:59.720
Then make writes the bytes, and load defines the class in a new class loader.

35
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.620
Kotlin uses the same builder.

36
00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:11.014
The one who handles each call is an interceptor: an object whose method runs in place of the original, and decides when to call it.

37
00:02:11.014 --> 00:02:13.797
Byte Buddy reads its parameter annotations.

38
00:02:13.797 --> 00:02:16.516
@Origin Method is the method being called.

39
00:02:16.516 --> 00:02:20.400
@SuperCall Callable runs the original body when you call it.

40
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.284
Ours writes down the method's name, then calls the original.

41
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.149
If SuperCall had an honest name, it would be called: now do the real thing.

42
00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:31.629
Everything before it is your extra step.

43
00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:34.419
Calling it plays the role exactly as written.

44
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:37.412
So what did Byte Buddy hand back?

45
00:02:37.412 --> 00:02:39.994
Which methods does the new class declare?

46
00:02:39.994 --> 00:02:43.270
And will stage still answer, even though it's final?

47
00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.277
Pause and guess.

48
00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.666
Here's what the lesson's demo printed.

49
00:02:50.666 --> 00:02:55.639
The name ends in $ByteBuddy$ and a suffix that is random on every run.

50
00:02:55.867 --> 00:02:56.522
YUk6Nqln.

51
00:02:56.522 --> 00:03:04.963
Byte Buddy names its costumes the way a cat walks across a keyboard, and when I ran it again, I got a different cat.

52
00:03:04.963 --> 00:03:08.601
So the class is real, but never count on its name.

53
00:03:09.233 --> 00:03:15.981
It declares run and check, plus an accessor for each, a helper that calls super, as we did by hand.

54
00:03:15.981 --> 00:03:17.958
The SuperCall object uses it.

55
00:03:17.958 --> 00:03:19.935
And stage isn't there at all.

56
00:03:19.935 --> 00:03:22.252
Yet it still answered: main stage.

57
00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:25.679
That's because a final method can't be overridden.

58
00:03:25.679 --> 00:03:31.333
The subclass simply inherits it, so it runs as written, and no interceptor ever sees it.

59
00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:35.363
That's the one rule behind every subclass proxy.

60
00:03:35.363 --> 00:03:38.137
It can only intercept what it can override.

61
00:03:38.137 --> 00:03:40.653
A final class can't be extended at all.

62
00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.362
A final or private method runs as written.

63
00:03:43.362 --> 00:03:47.361
And every subclass object runs the superclass constructor too.

64
00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:49.271
Now the twist.

65
00:03:49.271 --> 00:03:54.224
In Kotlin, classes and their members are final unless you write open.

66
00:03:54.224 --> 00:03:59.893
The demo's last line asks Byte Buddy to sew a costume for a plain Kotlin class.

67
00:03:59.893 --> 00:04:00.826
What happens?

68
00:04:00.826 --> 00:04:01.975
Pause and guess.

69
00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:08.093
Cannot subclass primitive, array or final types.

70
00:04:08.093 --> 00:04:11.871
Kotlin locked the door, and Byte Buddy doesn't pick locks.

71
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.893
So write open on the class and on each method.

72
00:04:15.893 --> 00:04:17.977
Or let a compiler plugin do it.

73
00:04:17.977 --> 00:04:25.641
Kotlin's all-open plugin opens classes and their members at compile time, for classes with annotations you choose.

74
00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:28.518
Here the costume picture breaks.

75
00:04:28.518 --> 00:04:31.993
A costume only makes you look like the lighting operator.

76
00:04:31.993 --> 00:04:34.004
A subclass really is a CueRunner.

77
00:04:34.004 --> 00:04:38.028
Every caller treats it as one, so the calling code doesn't change.

78
00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:39.908
Now the names.

79
00:04:39.908 --> 00:04:45.616
This is a subclass proxy: a generated subclass that overrides methods to add work.

80
00:04:45.616 --> 00:04:49.306
The object that handles each call is the interceptor.

81
00:04:49.306 --> 00:04:52.229
Its helpers that call super are accessors.

82
00:04:53.133 --> 00:04:54.534
Three rules of thumb.

83
00:04:54.534 --> 00:04:59.334
Look for final first, on the class and on every method you want wrapped.

84
00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:03.268
In Kotlin, open the class and its members, or use all-open.

85
00:05:03.268 --> 00:05:09.203
And cglib, an older library for this job, says in its own README that it is unmaintained.

86
00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:13.707
So, can anything stand in for a class with no interface?

87
00:05:13.707 --> 00:05:15.813
Yes: a costume, sewn on the spot.

88
00:05:15.813 --> 00:05:21.111
Extend the class, override what you can, add a step, then play the role as written.

89
00:05:21.111 --> 00:05:23.472
Whatever is final stays out of reach.

90
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.212
One question is still open.

91
00:05:26.212 --> 00:05:27.689
Who wears the costume?

92
00:05:27.689 --> 00:05:33.260
Is the costume the actor, or a lookalike who passes each request to the real actor?

93
00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:37.355
That decides what happens to this.check, in the next section.

94
00:05:38.267 --> 00:05:39.073
Quick check.

95
00:05:39.073 --> 00:05:40.752
Make check final as well.

96
00:05:40.752 --> 00:05:44.111
What does the interceptor log for one call to run?

97
00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:48.724
Only intercepted run.

98
00:05:48.724 --> 00:05:54.353
Byte Buddy can't override check, so the self call runs the original check directly.
